    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go a bit deeper on the actual signaling mechanism? like how does the leucine actually sense the presence of the amino acid to kick off the mTOR complex? also, how does the energy status of the cell (like via AMPK) interact with that mTOR response during the surplus?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When leucine is sensed by muscle cells, it exerts its influence on the mTOR pathway by binding to and indirectly activating mTORC1, also known as the Ragulator-Rag complex. This complex acts as a sensor for amino acid availability. The availability of leucine appears to directly signal through plasma membrane amino acid transporters (LAT1), then it is quickly metabolized within the cytosol to activate a series of downstream signaling processes involving proteins like Rheb, TSC complex, and ultimately leading to mTORC1 activation, given the presence of sufficient ATP and two key eIF4 initiation factors (eIF4E and eIF4G).\n\n\nRegarding the interplay between mTOR and AMPK during a caloric surplus, the AMPK (AMP-activated protein kinase) is an energy-sensing enzyme that becomes activated in low-energy states, such as during caloric deficit. AMPK inhibits the mTOR pathway to conserve energy and increase amino acid and glucose uptake. However, during times of surplus, elevated cellular energy levels reduce AMPK activity, allowing mTOR to continue promoting anabolic processes such as protein synthesis. The regulation between these two pathways ensures that the energy status of the cell is woven into the decision to synthesize",
      "source": "target",
